From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Thu Jul  1 15:35:10 1999
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Thu, 01 Jul 1999 16:35:10 +0100
Subject: Re: [S] Does the new version of S-Plus fix num acc. and RNG problems?
In-Reply-To: <s77b3192.062@fcc.gov>
References: <s77b3192.062@fcc.gov>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.9907011619180.3665-100000@auk.stats>

On Thu, 1 Jul 1999, Bruce McCullough wrote:

> 
> 
> Professor Ripley writes:
> 
> >Which new version did you have in mind: S-PLUS 5.1 or 2000? >The RNG is
> >unchanged in both, and quite rightly so.
> 
> Not meaning to be contentious, I wish Professor
> Ripley would please explain why this is "quite rightly so".

My opinion, of course.

> Ripley (1990) suggests that an RNG, among
> other things, (1) have a very long period and (2) produce
> close to independent output in a moderate number
> of dimensions.  The S-Plus RNG does neither of
> these.  Why do (1) and (2) not matter for S-PLUS?

Because the effect of changing the PRNG in S-PLUS would be to make previous
results irreproducible, and that seems to me to be a very major
disincentive to change it unless it is really inadequate. There was a
considerable outcry at the last change (about 3.2).

I would dispute that the S-PLUS PRNG does not do (2). It depends of what
you mean by `close' and by 'moderate', but this version of SUPER-DUPER
looks pretty good on those aspects on both theoretical and empirical tests.

For (1), a long period has to measured relative to the speed of the
program: to run through the cycle of the S-PLUS PRNG took several hours
last time I tried it, and so to do a useful simulation that needs around
one period would need several days, if it did not run out of other
resources (which it almost certainly would). People do not in general do
such things in S-PLUS, and if they do they are at liberty to add their own
generator.  (I actually ran through the cycele to compare the output with a
C version coded from the V&R3 description: they did agree.)

Summary: I think the S-PLUS PRNG is perfectly adequate for the usual
purposes of S-PLUS, and that to change it would be a very major upheaval
for some users.  There would be some considerable danger that a
replacement was actually worse on the aspects important to any one user
(as distinct from designers of empirical tests).  Some of the generators in
common use were actually designed to pass the tests in common use, which
rather invalidates those tests.

One way out would be to allow users to select between different
underlying PRNGs, even to link in their own. That has been the approach of
another S-like language.

-- 
