From BMCCULLO at fcc.gov  Thu Jul  1 16:07:14 1999
From: BMCCULLO at fcc.gov (Bruce McCullough)
Date: Thu, 01 Jul 1999 12:07:14 -0400
Subject: Re: [S] Does the new version of S-Plus fix num acc. and RNG problems?
In-Reply-To: <s77b3192.062@fcc.gov>
Message-ID: <s77b5a17.061@fcc.gov>

Many thanks to Professor Professor Ripley
for clarifying his position.  For those of you
who are unaware, Professor Ripley wrote
one of "The Books" realted to the use of RNGs
(Stochastic Simulation,  Wiley, 1987).  So if
he is satisfied with the S-PLUS "modification"
of the SUPER-DUPER RNG, that certainly
lessens my discomfiture.  But I am discomfited
nonetheless.

>Because the effect of changing the PRNG in S-PLUS would be >to make previous 
>results irreproducible, and that seems to me to >be a very major disincentive 
>to change it unless it is really >inadequate. 

This is indeed a legitimate objection.  One solution, adopted
by the econometrics package TSP, is not to replace the
old generator, but add a new one, so that users can choose
between the old and the new.

>I would dispute that the S-PLUS PRNG does not do (2). It >depends of what you 
>mean by `close' and by 'moderate', but this >version of SUPER-DUPER looks 
>pretty good on those aspects >on both theoretical and empirical tests.

As far as empirical tests go, I imagine that the S-PLUS "modified" super duper 
might well pass the Knuth tests.  But for theoretical
tests, I am unaware of any such analysis of the S-PLUS RNG.
There are such analyses of the real SUPER-DUPER RNG,
but not the "modified" version employed by S-PLUS.

>For (1), a long period has to measured relative to the speed of >the program: 
>to run through the cycle of the S-PLUS PRNG took >several hours [snip]

This point is most telling.  If the failures were limited to
the rightmost bits (say, 31 and 32), it would make
sense.  However, S-PLUS fails the OQSO test (Overlapping
Quadruples Sparse Occupancy) for bits 17-32, suggesting
that only the fist 16 bits are uncorrelated, and 2^16
is about 65000.   So, it appears to me, the question isn't how long does it 
take to cycle through the entire generator, but how long
does it take to use 65000 (assuming the user takes steps
to ensure that only the first 16 bits are used).

This is aside from the opinions of Ripley (1987) and
Knuth (1997) that, at most, only a fraction of the 
RNG's period be used.  If this is true in the present
case.  Even a modest use of the
S-PLUS RNG will quickly run through a fraction of 
65000.

I remain unpersuaded that S-PLUS should not 
offer users a better RNG.

Of course, anybody who really cares about this
can implement a good long period RNG in
FORTRAN and call it.

Bruce

