On Thu, 1 Jul 1999, Bruce McCullough wrote:

> As far as empirical tests go, I imagine that the S-PLUS "modified" super 
> duper might well pass the Knuth tests.  But for theoretical
> tests, I am unaware of any such analysis of the S-PLUS RNG.
> There are such analyses of the real SUPER-DUPER RNG,
> but not the "modified" version employed by S-PLUS.

As Marsaglia has never AFAIK published Super-Duper, but called two
different things by that name, which is the real one? I am aware of
theoretical analyses for the original one (almost as used in S-PLUS),
but not of the one apparently fixed up to pass DIEHARD.

> sense.  However, S-PLUS fails the OQSO test (Overlapping
> Quadruples Sparse Occupancy) for bits 17-32, suggesting

As the S-PLUS code does not treat the RNG as a random bit generator,
I think tests of particular sets of low-order bits are irrelevant.
To use just the bits 17-32 would be appalling programming practice.

> that only the fist 16 bits are uncorrelated, and 2^16
> is about 65000.   So, it appears to me, the question isn't how long does
it
> take to cycle through the entire generator, but how long
> does it take to use 65000 (assuming the user takes steps
> to ensure that only the first 16 bits are used).

That is a non-sequiteur. The first 16 bits have a very much longer cycle
than 65000, indeed the period of the whole sequence.

> This is aside from the opinions of Ripley (1987) and
> Knuth (1997) that, at most, only a fraction of the 
> RNG's period be used.  If this is true in the present

Well of course I know that. My bet is that no user of S-PLUS has ever used
more than 10% of the period in a real simulation.

