Here are (what I hope are) the correct timing results:

test2.s
a <- unix.time(runif(10000000,0,1))
print(a);

test2.out
S-PLUS : Copyright (c) 1988, 1999 MathSoft, Inc.
S : Copyright Lucent Technologies, Inc.
Version 5.1 Release 1 for Linux 2.0.31 : 1999 
Working data will be in .Data 
> a <- unix.time(runif(10000000,0,1))
> print(a);
[1] 3.44 0.42 4.00 0.00 0.00

>

Assuming two processors and using user time (3.44 sec):

Generating 10^10 numbers would take about (10^10/(2*10^7)*3.44/60/60/24)
0.01990741 user time days (under the nutty linearity assumption), 10^16
would take about 19907 days, and 10^18 would take about 1990741 days.

Assuming two processors and using system time (0.42 sec):

Generating 10^10 numbers would take about (10^10/(2*10^7)*0.42/60/60/24)
0.002430556 system time days (under the nutty linearity assumption), 10^16
would take about 2430.556 days, and 10^18 would take about 243055.6 days.

One can quickly see that drawing 10^10 random numbers in Splus is certainly
feasible, but drawing 10^16 let alone 10^18 really isn't.

Jas.

