From donald at portal.ca  Sat Jul  3 12:54:33 1999
From: donald at portal.ca (Alan Donald)
Date: Sat, 03 Jul 1999 05:54:33 -0700
Subject: Re: [S] Generating correlated binary random variables
Message-ID: <199907031254.FAA12941@pop3.portal.ca>

>
>Hi,
>
>Does anyone out there have or knows of a S-Plus function that generates
>correlated binary rv's. Obviously there are many dependence structures and
>therefore many possible functions.
>
>Any information will be helpful to me at this point, thanks,
>
>Ori

        Sorry about the week's delay in a response here.  I just got back
from a week or so away.

        You might try the following, which generates binary responses in
clusters.  That is the kind of thing you get when your subjects come in
groupos, litters or herds or families.  Or when you are getting multiple
responses from the same subject.

        For each cluster, generate a random beta variable p (between 0 and
1) from the beta distribution with parameters a and b (more about those later).

        This gives you a binomial probability parameter that you can use
with rbinom with parameters (1, 1, p) to generate a Bernouilli random
variable for the response of an individual in the cluster.  You maintain the
same "p" for all members of the cluster.  If all you want is the number of
postive responses  among the m members of the cluster, use rbinom(1, m, p). 

        This should be fairly straightforward to program and can be made
more efficient than the way I have described it.

        The characteristics of the data are as follows.

        1.  The expectation of a postive response is pi = a/(a + b)

        2.  a.  The covariance between two Bernouilli responses in the same
cluster is pi(1 - pi)* rho, where rho = 1/(1 + a + b).

        (rho is like the intraclass correlation coefficient in random
effects models.)

            b.   The covariance between two Bernouilli responses in
different clusters is 0.

        3.  a. Let Z1 and Z2 be two responses from the same cluster.  Then

              Pr(Z2 = 1 | Z1 = 1) = pi + rho*(1 - pi)

            b.  Let Z1 and Z2 be two responses from different clusters.  Then

                Pr(Z2 = 1 | Z1 = 1) = pi 

           

        Hope that helps.

Cheers

Alan

