Subject:        [S] Creating dataframes from lists

I am running a rather large dataset (24560 cases) using the 'by' and 'lm'
functions to calculate coefficients by different
breaks of the data.  At this time I am outputting to a text file.  I will
need to create a dataframe (or matrix) from the
results of this analysis.  If I output the results to a list 

(i.e results <- by(data,list(a,b,c),function(data)lm(d~e))

I would suggest putting the data frame inside the call to lm(), 

*       results <- by(data, list(a, b, c), function(dat) lm(d ~ e, dat)

        how can I coerce the list into a dataframe (or matrix)?  I might add
that I do have NULL results where no coefficients are 
        calculated for some breaks.

        pdd$Subject:34
        pdd$VisualField:Left VF
        pdd$Complexity:6 pt
        pdd$SameDifferent:Different
        pdd$StimulusType:20
        Call:
        lm(formula = pdd$lrt ~ pdd$ltime)

        Coefficients:
         (Intercept)  pdd$ltime
            7.078379 -0.3850314

        Degrees of freedom: 5 total; 3 residual
        Residual standard error: 0.6270569

        to

        Subject  VisualField Complexity SameDifferent StimulusType Intercept
Slope     ResError
         34         Left VF    6 pt        Different     20        7.078379
-.3850314   0.6270569

        The idea is to set up the factor part and the coefficient part
separately and then to put them together.  The factor part is easy:

*       res.factors <- expand.grid(dimnames(results))

        To get the coefficient part you need to be a bit more careful.
lapply(results, coef) will extract he coefficient vectors as a simple list,
but you want to signal missing vectors by NA rather than NULL so that they
are not discarded (which would muck up the correspondence with the factor
part):  Here is one way:

*       res.coeffs <- lapply(results, function(x) if(is.null(x)) NA else
coef(x))
*       res.coeffs <- do.call("rbind", res.coeffs)  ## this is the tricky
part!
*       all.coeffs <- cbind(res.factors, res.coeffs)

The result of the do.call is a matrix but cbind-ing it with a data frame
coerces it to data frame as well.

I would suggest you check that the results of expand.grid() and lapply()
really do match up.

Thanks 

Lance

