One of the things that you should try is to put some output in your BATCH
file.    What your are seeing on the "time" command is what it takes to
start the Batch file, not run it.  Try your same command with a
"unix.time(a_runif(100000000,0,1))" at the command line and see what your
answer is.  Also, try putting a "proc.time()" in your file to see what the
time used was.  I think that it will agree with what I was saying.

If you really did create 4*10^7 random numbers in 0.02 second, that says
each number was generate in 0.5 nanoseconds.  That implies a clock speed
around 2,000MHZ.  Now you are having to go through a loop and executing
several instructions, plus accessing memory, which is a lot slower.

Try it again and see what you come up with.  Here is the output off my
machine with the "proc.time()" calls.

