      Holtman/CIMG/CVG)
Subject:  Re: [S] Does the new version of S-Plus fix num acc. and RNG
      problems?

>Now someone must have a real fast computer.  I am running on a 300MHZ SUN
>and to generate 100,000,000 random numbers (a <- runif(10000000)) take
>about 50 seconds or 0.5 us/number.

This is becoming extraordinarily silly and tedious.  But someone must have
an extremely slow machine:

test2.s is:

a _ runif(10000000,0,1)
b _ runif(10000000,0,1)

>time Splus BATCH test2.s test2.out
>0.020u 0.020s 0:00.02 100.0%    0+0k 0+0io 279pf+0w

Since I have another processor, I ran the same command on it.  Therefore, I
have generated 4*10^7 random numbers in what the clock tells me is about
00.02 processor seconds.  This is far too small a number for the time
command to give useful resolution.  But that does imply that if the
generation of other numbers goes up linearly (which it doesn't), the time
needed to generate 10^16 numbers (which is about what I needed) takes:
10^16/(4*10^7)*(00.02/60)/60/60/24 = 0.9645062 processor days.

This exercise is obviously silly because (1) the time reported by the time
command is far TOO small to give useful resolution and (2) the time taken
to generate additional numbers does not go up linearly.

This test machine is a dual Xeon processor running LINUX.

Everyone reasonable agrees that the period of the Splus RNG is extremely
long.  But I, and others, worry about the serial correlation in the numbers
being generated.  When I ask for I.I.D. numbers, I need to get the closest
approximation to I.I.D. a computer can give.

Now I will go use my "real [sic] fast computer" to do some real work.

Jas.

