I have a follow up question to Nicole's question about renaming objects in one
step.

I have been using a function that copies an object to a different directory and
then deletes the original.  A similiar two step operation encompassed by one
command.  (Lazy perhaps, but laziness is a big reason I program and use a
computer!)  Given the responses to Nicole's question I am concerned my program
has safety issues that are not apparent to me.

move.function <- function(x, to = 3, from = 1)
{
   if(!is.character(x))
     stop("x must be character")
   if(!is.function(eval(parse(text = x))))
     warning(paste(x, "is not a function"))
   for(i in x)
     assign(i, eval(parse(text = i)), w = to)
   remove(x, w = from)
}

Only "completed" functions get moved to search()[3] (my func directory attached
in my .First statement).
This serves two purposes: one stop shopping for completed functions and fall
back revision control for changes.

I have found this function useful and would appreciate insight into
*  why (or if) it is unsafe, and
*  how it could be made safer.

Thanks, KJJ

intro to Nicole's summary

In regards to my posting yesterday about renaming objects in Splus
without having to go through the 2-step process of reassigning to a
new object then removing the old:

I had a number of objects to rename and was curious (and I admit a bit
lazy) if there was an easy one-step process to do so for each object.
It seems there is neither a built-in function nor a simple (safe) solution.
As Marc Feldesman wrote,  "The short answer is 'no'".

A few  people contributed some simple remedies but claimed no
libability if anything went wrong.  The first is from Patrick Connolly
the second from Alfred Balch and the latter from a user who wished to
remain "Anonymous" :-)

Prof. Ripley commented that "Note that all the suggestions _are_ two-step
ones, so do not seem to me to answer the question."

I agree with that assessment but, alas, my true motive was only having to
type the desired command once :-]

