Subject: [S] SPLUS 2000 glm problems
Message-ID: <4.1.19990707101718.00a9fc20@poptop.llnl.gov>

I seem to have uncovered a bug in the SPLUS 2000 glm support routines. (Is
this the first SPLUS 2000 bug reported, and if so, do I win a prize? :-)

The glm.weight()  function was changed in such a way that it causes
problems for non-standard mean or variance functions. (By nonstandard I
mean those that aren't enumerated in glm.weights.)  This change manifests
itself in such things as totally screwy SEs for effects computed by MASS's
glm.nb().  More generally, if you're building your own families, you should
pay careful attention to what gets put into the weights slot.

