From feldesmanm at pdx.edu  Thu Jul  8 19:34:37 1999
From: feldesmanm at pdx.edu (Marc R. Feldesman)
Date: Thu, 08 Jul 1999 12:34:37 -0700
Subject: [S] Comparing columns elements in matrices
Message-ID: <4.2.0.58.19990708115824.009cbf00@pop4.ibm.net>

I want to compare the results of tests that predict group membership, with 
actual membership.  I've done 8 different analyses, each of which predict 
membership in a known group.  Originally I had all of the results in a 
single matrix where column 1 contained the actual group membership, and 
columns 2 - 9 had the predicted results from each of the 8 tests.  What I 
want to do is to compare actual with result 1, actual with result 2, ..., 
actual with result 8, placing a 1 where there was agreement and a 0 where 
there was disagreement.  Simple enough.

I was unable to figure out how to do this more simply than the two options 
below:

resultmat<-apply(results, 2, function(x,y) ifelse(y==x, 1, 0), actual)

or

resultmat<-apply(results[,-1], 2, function(x,y) ifelse(y==x, 1, 0), results[,1]

Is there a way to do this that doesn't require the y argument (i.e. 
something like:

resultmat<-apply(results, 2, function(x) .....?)

I couldn't figure out anything simpler, but I'm still not fully fluent in 
the ways of apply().

A related issue arises from this.  After solving the problem above, I want 
to tabulate all the results pairwise.  I want table(method1, method2), 
table(method1, method3)..., until eventually I have all the possible pairs 
of tables.

This, again, appears to a solution in need of apply(),  but it also seems 
trickier to do since I need to do some indexing either implicitly or 
explicitly.  I need 1:2, 1:3, 1:4, 1:5, 1:6, 1:7, 1:8, 2:3, 2:4...., 7:8

Of course I can do this with explicit looping but I'm always trying to take 
advantage of the vectorization wherever possible.

Is there some slick apply family trick that will get me this?

As always, thank you very much for your help.

Dr. Marc R. Feldesman
