From feldesmanm at pdx.edu  Thu Jul  8 21:36:18 1999
From: feldesmanm at pdx.edu (Marc R. Feldesman)
Date: Thu, 08 Jul 1999 14:36:18 -0700
Subject: Re: [S] Comparing columns elements in matrices
In-Reply-To: <4.2.0.58.19990708115824.009cbf00@pop4.ibm.net>
References: <4.2.0.58.19990708115824.009cbf00@pop4.ibm.net>
Message-ID: <4.2.0.58.19990708142743.00a12ba0@pop4.ibm.net>

Thanks to Rolf Turner, Scott Chaslow, Claude Nadeau, and Steve McKinney for 
sending me helpful responses to my questions concerning columnwise comparisons.

My initial response to some of the answers was "Duh, of course I knew 
that"; however as the answers became more varied, I realized just how 
infinitely malleable S-Plus really is.

Some responses:

>From Scott Chaslow:

>I think you should forget about apply() for this entirely, and take
>advantage of implicit vector expansion, as in:
>
>resultmat <- results == actual
>
>Here, results is n x p and actual is n x 1 (or an n-length vector).
>Object actual gets expanded to have the same length as object
>results, equivalent to rep(actual, ncol(results)), and then the
>element-by-element comparison is done in one fell swoop.  If you
>prefer 0,1 to F,T, just do mode(resultmat) <- "numeric"
>(resultmat <- as.numeric(resultmat) would lose the dimensions),
>although leaving the result as "logical" may make the results of
>the next step slightly more readable.

>From Steve McKinney:

>You could get the 0/1 concordance data this way with matrix operations
>only, always the fastest and least memory intensive in Splus.  apply()
>is slower, and a memory hog, holding piles of memory until all calcs
>are done.
>
>
>actualvec <- c(1,1,1,2,3,2,2,3,1,2,3,3)
>pred1vec  <- c(1,2,1,2,3,2,1,3,2,3,3,2)
>pred2vec  <- c(1,1,1,2,1,2,2,3,2,1,1,2)
>pred3vec  <- c(3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3)
>pred4vec  <- c(1,3,2,2,3,2,3,2,1,1,2,1)
>
>pred.mat<-cbind(
> pred1vec,
> pred2vec,
> pred3vec,
> pred4vec
>)
>actual.mat<-matrix(actualvec,nrow=length(actualvec),ncol=ncol(pred.mat),byro
>w=F)
>
>pred.mat
>      pred1vec pred2vec pred3vec pred4vec
> [1,]        1        1        3        1
> [2,]        2        1        3        3
> [3,]        1        1        3        2
> [4,]        2        2        3        2
> [5,]        3        1        3        3
> [6,]        2        2        3        2
> [7,]        1        2        3        3
> [8,]        3        3        3        2
> [9,]        2        2        3        1
>[10,]        3        1        3        1
>[11,]        3        1        3        2
>[12,]        2        2        3        1
>
>
>actual.mat
>
>      [,1] [,2] [,3] [,4]
> [1,]    1    1    1    1
> [2,]    1    1    1    1
> [3,]    1    1    1    1
> [4,]    2    2    2    2
> [5,]    3    3    3    3
> [6,]    2    2    2    2
> [7,]    2    2    2    2
> [8,]    3    3    3    3
> [9,]    1    1    1    1
>[10,]    2    2    2    2
>[11,]    3    3    3    3
>[12,]    3    3    3    3
>
>concordance.mat<-1.0*(pred.mat==actual.mat)
>concordance.mat
>
>      pred1vec pred2vec pred3vec pred4vec
> [1,]        1        1        0        1
> [2,]        0        1        0        0
> [3,]        1        1        0        0
> [4,]        1        1        0        1
> [5,]        1        0        1        1
> [6,]        1        1        0        1
> [7,]        0        1        0        0
> [8,]        1        1        1        0
> [9,]        0        0        0        1
>[10,]        0        0        0        0
>[11,]        1        0        1        0
>[12,]        0        0        1        0

>From Claude Nadeau:

>How about something that does not require apply.
>If mat is your "original" matrix (with 9 columns)
>  mat[,-1]==mat[,1]
>basically does what you want.
>
>If you want 0's and 1's instead of F's and T's, just wrap it in ifelse
>  ifelse(mat[,-1]==mat[,1],1,0)

>From Rolf Turner:

>Doesn't
>
>> resultmat <- 0 + (result[,1]==result[,-1])
>
>solve your first problem?

Scott Chaslow offered a solution to the second problem:

>I *would* use lapply for this one, but maybe not quite how you
>expect.  I would apply it to a list of column indices, not
>to resultmat.  The only mildly tricky bit is getting the pairs
>of column indices.  You need all combinations of 8 things taken
>2 at a time.  For this, I use a function in my combinat library:
>combn().  I include this (and a required auxiliary function) below
>(the library is available at http://www.spg.wau.nl/pv/pub/chasalow/S ).
>
>I use simplify = F in combn to be sure to get a list instead of
>a matrix of column indices, and use lapply instead of apply.
>This is to guarantee that the result is a list;  apply() sometimes
>returns a vector or matrix, sometimes a list (undocumented), depending
>on conformability of the results.
>
>out <- lapply(combn(8, 2, simplify = F), function(i, x)
>    table(x[, i[1]], x[, i[2]]), x = resultmat)
>names(out) <- apply(combn(8, 2), 2, paste, collapse=" ")
>
>A cute, and probably handier alternative (if a bit more work),
>is to use tabulate instead of table, thus forcing every comparison
>to return 4 values.  Then you can put them in a matrix, which usually
>is easier to view:
>
>out <- apply(combn(8, 2), 2, function(i, x)
>    tabulate(match(paste(x[, i[1]], x[, i[2]]), paste(c(T,T,F,F),
>c(T,F,T,F))),
>    nbins = 4), x = resultmat)
>dimnames(out) <- list(paste(c(T,T,F,F), c(T,F,T,F), sep ="/"),
>    apply(combn(8, 2), 2, paste,    collapse="/"))
>out <- t(out)

Thanks again for your help.  Although there are similarities in some of the 
answers to the first problem, I am again amazed at the variety.

Dr. Marc R. Feldesman
