Hi. I have a very weird tree problem both in "Version 4.0 Release 3 for
Microsoft Windows : 1997" and in "License Managed Professional Edition
Version 4.5 Release 2 for Microsoft Windows : 1998."

I use the data below. (This is a subset, as small as I've been able to make
it and still see this problem, from the "wine" data available at the
U.C.-Irvine data repository.) The significant digits matter, too: if you
round everything by one more digit, the problem goes away. Note that the
first column is categorical; this is a classification tree.

I've called the data "m7." I create a tree in the usual way with
> m7.tree <- tree (Y ~ ., data = m7)

and I print it out. So far, so good. Note the numbers of items in the
leaves: 6, 5, and 9.

> m7.tree
node), split, n, deviance, yval, (yprob)
      * denotes terminal node

1) root 20 41.950 2 ( 0.3500 0.4500 0.2000 )  
  2) Alcohol<12.8901 11 10.430 2 ( 0.0000 0.8182 0.1818 )  
    4) Alcohol<12.3222 6  0.000 2 ( 0.0000 1.0000 0.0000 ) *
    5) Alcohol>12.3222 5  6.730 2 ( 0.0000 0.6000 0.4000 ) *
  3) Alcohol>12.8901 9  9.535 1 ( 0.7778 0.0000 0.2222 ) *

The frame agrees: the numbers of items in the leaves are 6, 5, and 9. 

> m7.tree$frame
      var  n       dev yval splits.cutleft splits.cutright   yprob.1
yprob.2   yprob.3 
1 Alcohol 20 41.946152    2       <12.8901        >12.8901 0.3500000
0.4500000 0.2000000
2 Alcohol 11 10.431065    2       <12.3222        >12.3222 0.0000000
0.8181818 0.1818182
4  <leaf>  6  0.000000    2                                0.0000000
1.0000000 0.0000000
5  <leaf>  5  6.730117    2                                0.0000000
0.6000000 0.4000000
3  <leaf>  9  9.534712    1                                0.7777778
0.0000000 0.2222222

However, the "where" element of the tree doesn't agree. It has 6, 4, and 10:

> table (m7.tree$where)
 3 4  5 
 6 4 10

It is almost always the case that when I see this disagreement, I am then
unable to prune the tree. (Of course, in this case I might not want to
prune, but this data is drawn from a much larger data set where I would want
to.)

> prune.tree (m7.tree)
Error in .C("VR_prune2",: subroutine VR_prune2: 1 Inf value(s) in argument 5
Dumped

It's possible that disagreement between "where" and "frame$n" isn't the
cause of the "prune" problem, because every once in a while I can still
prune a "disagreeing" tree. But generally this sort of disagreement stops
"prune" in its tracks. 

Has anyone ever seen this disagreement? Or had trees that "prune" wouldn't
work on?

Thanks for any advice,

