From ecm at casbah.acns.nwu.edu  Sat Jul 10 17:17:49 1999
From: ecm at casbah.acns.nwu.edu (Edward Malthouse)
Date: Sat, 10 Jul 1999 12:17:49 -0500
Subject: Re: [S] strip labels for shingles in xyplot()
In-Reply-To: <37875D20.A28229B@telecomplus.sn>
References: <37875D20.A28229B@telecomplus.sn>
Message-ID: <199907101717.MAA04592@casbah.acns.nwu.edu>

I have a solution to the problem below and a related question for
other S-news readers. 

> I would like to print some meaningful text as a label for a shingle (for
> example, "Light", "Medium" or "Heavy"), in place of its name and the
> coloured bar indicating the interval order. I fiddled with
> strip.default() but could not anything but error messages. Does someone
> know the trick ?

First the solution.  You can turn off the shading in the strip labels with
strip=function(...) strip.default(..., style=1)

To place text in the strip plots you can use a "levels" attr,
e.g., compare the following:
> dat <- list(x=runif(100), y=runif(100), z=c(rep(1,50), rep(2,50)))
> xyplot(y~x|z, data=dat)
> attr(dat$z, "levels") <- c("Low", "High")
> xyplot(y~x|z, data=dat, strip=function(...) strip.default(..., style=1))

Now for my question:

Why does trellis put the shading in the strip plots?  Here are
some reasons why the shading is a bad idea:

1.  The shading gives a visual cue that the variable represented
in the strip plots falls on a continuum.  If the variable is
categorical this cue is misleading.

2.  If the variable is metric, where are the tick marks?  If the
point is to encode the value of variable in the strip plot as a
visual object, how is the reader supposed to decode it?  I am
tempted to use the tick marks on the horizontal axis, but these
are for the "x" variable, not the strip variable.

3.  The shading doesn't xerox particularly well.

4.  My non-statistician colleagues complain about the shading and
ask me to remove it.

Are there any good reasons to place shading in the strips?  Do any
s-news readers prefer shading over not having shading?  Why is
shading the default and why isn't discussion of how to turn it off
included in the S-plus Trellis graphics user manual.  (I learned
how to suppress it from the s-news archive).

Ed Malthouse

